 "executive_read": "The alt-AM group is in the grip of a private-credit liquidity test, and it is sorting the names by the one variable that was supposed to make them safe: the quality of their permanent capital. Blue Owl \u2014 90% perpetual, the highest in the group \u2014 is also the worst performer of 2026, down roughly 18%, because its permanent capital is retail semi-liquid credit now facing redemption requests that forced gating at two funds. That is the paradox this dashboard is built to expose: not all perpetual capital is equal, and the market is repricing the difference. The genuinely durable engines are the insurance-linked spread platforms \u2014 Apollo's Athene above all, with KKR's Global Atlantic scaling behind it \u2014 where liabilities are contractual annuities, not redeemable fund units. Fee-related earnings grew across nearly every name in FY2025; the question for 2026 is no longer growth but the redemption-resilience of the capital base behind it.",
 "bottom_line": "On quality-adjusted fundamentals we rank the insurance-linked compounders (APO, KKR) at the top: contractual spread liabilities are the most redemption-proof capital in the group, and APO trades at the cheapest large-cap multiple despite it. Blue Owl is the sharpest disagreement between our quality score and the market \u2014 screen-cheap with the highest perpetual share, but only if its retail credit base stabilises; until redemptions normalise it is a falling knife, not a value entry. Carlyle and StepStone screen lowest on durability and we would not reach for either on valuation alone. This is an analytical judgement, not a recommendation; all figures are FY2025 reported or illustrative.",
